<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Projection Calculator</h2>
  <p>A projection in a right triangle is the shadow a leg casts onto the hypotenuse. Drop the altitude from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse, and it splits that side into two segments, called p and q. Segment p belongs to leg a, and segment q belongs to leg b.</p>
  <p>This Right Triangle Projection Calculator works with all five projection relationships. It finds projection p from leg a, projection q from leg b, leg a from projection p, leg b from projection q, and the full hypotenuse from p and q. Each task is a separate mode, so you pick the one that matches the measurements you already know.</p>
  <p>The same two projections can also rebuild the whole triangle. For example, if <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9 + 16 = 25]]')} /> cm, and the altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{9 \\times 16} = 12]]')} /> cm. This guide explains each mode, shows the formulas, works through examples, and teaches you how to check every answer.</p>
</section>

