<!-- Section 3: sec-what-projections -->
<section id="sec-what-projections" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are Projections in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Take right triangle ABC, with the right angle at C. The two sides that form the right angle are the legs, AC and BC. The side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse, AB. Now draw the altitude from C straight to the hypotenuse, meeting it at a point called D. That altitude is perpendicular to the hypotenuse, so it meets AB at a right angle.</p>
  <p>Point D splits the hypotenuse into two adjacent segments. The segment next to vertex A, called AD, is projection p. The segment next to vertex B, called DB, is projection q. Together they make up the whole hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p + q = c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each projection belongs to the leg that touches its vertex. Leg a touches vertex A, so its projection is p. Leg b touches vertex B, so its projection is q. This pairing never changes: a goes with p, and b goes with q.</p>
  <p>Projections lie flat along the hypotenuse. They are not sides of the triangle, and they are not perpendicular to anything. Picture each one as the shadow its leg casts onto the hypotenuse when the altitude drops straight down to it.</p>
</section>

