<!-- Section 5: sec-formulas -->
<section id="sec-formulas" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Projection Formulas Explained</h2>
  <p>Five formulas cover every mode on this page. The first two find projections from legs. The next two recover legs from projections. The last one rebuilds the hypotenuse from its two parts.</p>
  <p>Projection p, from leg a and the hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p = \\frac{a^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Projection q, from leg b and the hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Leg a, from the hypotenuse and projection p:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Leg b, from the hypotenuse and projection q:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Hypotenuse, from both projections:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Finding a projection means squaring a leg and dividing by the hypotenuse. Finding a leg means multiplying the hypotenuse by a projection and taking a square root. Finding the hypotenuse means adding two segments. The square root appears only when recovering a leg, because only the leg formulas start from a squared length. All measurements must use the same unit, so convert before you calculate.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-choose-mode -->
<section id="sec-choose-mode" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Choose the Correct Calculator Mode</h2>
  <p>Choosing the mode is a matching exercise: find the mode that asks for the two measurements you know, and the calculator returns the missing one.</p>
  <h3>Mode 1: Hypotenuse Segment p</h3>
  <p>Choose this mode when you know leg a and hypotenuse c. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[p = a^2 / c]]')} />. Square leg a, then divide by c. Use it when a problem gives you a leg and the whole hypotenuse and asks how much of the hypotenuse belongs to that leg.</p>
  <h3>Mode 2: Hypotenuse Segment q</h3>
  <p>Choose this mode when you know leg b and hypotenuse c. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[q = b^2 / c]]')} />. It is the mirror image of mode 1, with leg b in place of leg a. Use it when the known leg is the one touching vertex B.</p>
  <h3>Mode 3: Leg a Squared Projection</h3>
  <p>Choose this mode when you know hypotenuse c and projection p. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} />. Multiply first, then take the square root. Use it when a problem gives the hypotenuse and the segment next to A and asks for the leg.</p>
  <h3>Mode 4: Leg b Squared Projection</h3>
  <p>Choose this mode when you know hypotenuse c and projection q. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]')} />. It is the mirror image of mode 3, with q in place of p. Use it when the known segment is the one next to B.</p>
  <h3>Mode 5: Projections Sum to c</h3>
  <p>Choose this mode when you know both projections, p and q. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />, a plain addition with no squaring and no square root.</p>
  <p>Quick decision guide: a leg with c gives a projection, c with a projection gives a leg, and both projections together give c. If your known values match none of these pairs, you need another measurement first.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
