</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-projection-p -->
<section id="sec-projection-p" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Projection P From Leg A and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Projection p is the part of the hypotenuse that belongs to leg a. The formula divides the square of leg a by the full hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p = \\frac{a^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm. Step 1 writes the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[p = a^2 / c]]')} />. Step 2 substitutes the values, <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9^2 / 15 = 81 / 15]]')} />. Step 3 divides, giving <span set:html={rich('[[p = 5.4]]')} /> cm. The answer is a length along the hypotenuse, measured in the same unit as a and c.</p>
  <p>A short check confirms the result. The other leg in this triangle is 12 cm, so its projection is <span set:html={rich('[[q = 12^2 / 15 = 144 / 15 = 9.6]]')} /> cm. Adding the two projections gives <span set:html={rich('[[5.4 + 9.6 = 15]]')} /> cm, which is exactly c. If your two projections do not add to c, one of the inputs was wrong. The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-p-calculator/">projection p calculator</a> handles this calculation on its own when you need it often.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-p-leg-a.svg')} alt="Right triangle showing leg a squared divided by hypotenuse c gives projection p, with a equals 9 cm and c equals 15 cm giving p equals 5.4 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Projection p from leg a: p equals a squared over c, so 81 over 15 gives 5.4 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-projection-q -->
<section id="sec-projection-q" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Projection Q From Leg B and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Projection q is the part of the hypotenuse that belongs to leg b. The formula divides the square of leg b by the full hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm. Step 1 writes the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[q = b^2 / c]]')} />. Step 2 substitutes the values, <span set:html={rich('[[q = 12^2 / 15 = 144 / 15]]')} />. Step 3 divides, giving <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9.6]]')} /> cm. The answer is the segment next to vertex B, on the opposite side of the altitude foot from p.</p>
  <p>Compare the two projections before moving on. Here p is 5.4 cm and q is 9.6 cm, so q is larger, which matches leg b being longer than leg a. The longer leg always casts the longer projection. If your longer leg produced the shorter projection, you swapped p and q. The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-q-calculator/">projection q calculator</a> handles this calculation on its own when you need it often.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-leg-b.svg')} alt="Right triangle showing leg b squared divided by hypotenuse c gives projection q, with b equals 12 cm and c equals 15 cm giving q equals 9.6 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Projection q from leg b: q equals b squared over c, so 144 over 15 gives 9.6 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-leg-a -->
<section id="sec-leg-a" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Leg A From Projection P</h2>
  <p>To recover leg a, multiply the hypotenuse by projection p and take the square root:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm. The product is <span set:html={rich('[[25 \\times 9 = 225]]')} />, and the square root of 225 is 15, so <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} /> cm. The product 225 is a squared length, not a side, which is why the square root cannot be skipped. Reporting 225 as the leg would claim a side nine times longer than the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The square root step is the whole point of this mode. The projection theorem says <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} />, so the product gives the square of the leg. Undoing a square needs a square root, just as undoing a doubling needs halving. The <a href="/right-triangle-leg-a-from-projection-calculator/">leg a from projection calculator</a> covers this calculation in full when you need it on its own.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-leg-b -->
<section id="sec-leg-b" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Leg B From Projection Q</h2>
  <p>To recover leg b, multiply the hypotenuse by projection q and take the square root:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm. The product is <span set:html={rich('[[25 \\times 16 = 400]]')} />, and the square root of 400 is 20, so <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} /> cm. The method is the mirror image of the leg a calculation, with q in place of p. The pairing is fixed by the triangle labels, so entering p here would return leg a instead.</p>
  <p>Check the answer against the size of the triangle. Leg b must be shorter than the hypotenuse, and 20 cm is shorter than 25 cm, so the result passes the first sense check. Squaring it gives <span set:html={rich('[[20^2 = 400]]')} />, which matches <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times q = 25 \\times 16 = 400]]')} />, so the theorem holds exactly. The <a href="/right-triangle-leg-b-from-projection-calculator/">leg b from projection calculator</a> covers this calculation in full when you need it on its own.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Complete Hypotenuse From Projections</h2>
  <p>The two projections are the two parts of one straight side, so the hypotenuse is their sum:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm. Adding gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9 + 16 = 25]]')} /> cm. This is the segment addition principle: when a point divides a segment into two parts, the whole segment equals the sum of its parts. The altitude foot D divides the hypotenuse AB into AD and DB, so AB equals AD plus DB.</p>
  <p>This mode is also the fastest check on the other four modes. Whenever you calculate p and q separately, add them and compare with c. In the 9-12-15 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[p = 5.4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9.6]]')} /> cm add to 15 cm, confirming both projections at once. The <a href="/right-triangle-hypotenuse-from-projections-calculator/">hypotenuse from projections calculator</a> covers this calculation in full when you need it on its own.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-how-to-use -->
<section id="sec-how-to-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Projection Calculator</h2>
  <p>Ten short steps take you from a labeled diagram to a checked answer. The steps are the same for every mode; only the mode you pick and the numbers you enter change.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify which measurements are known.</li>
    <li>Open the calculator mode selector.</li>
    <li>Choose the matching calculation mode.</li>
    <li>Enter the first required measurement.</li>
    <li>Enter the second required measurement.</li>
    <li>Select compatible measurement units.</li>
    <li>Click the relevant Calculate button.</li>
    <li>Read the displayed result.</li>
    <li>Check the answer using another geometric relationship when appropriate.</li>
    <li>Use Reset before starting another calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Step 1 does most of the work. Label the triangle, mark p and q on the hypotenuse, and note which leg each projection belongs to. With the labels settled, the mode choice follows from the table near the top of this guide, and the calculator handles the arithmetic. Do not describe or expect features the page does not have: it does not calculate the altitude, the angles, or the area automatically.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Right Triangle Projection Diagram</h2>
  <p>The main diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg a, side BC is leg b, and side AB is the hypotenuse c. Altitude CD drops from C perpendicular to AB and touches it at D. Two small squares mark the right angles: one at C, between the legs, and one at D, between the altitude and the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Point D splits the hypotenuse into segment AD, which is projection p, and segment DB, which is projection q. The altitude CD is labeled h. Trace the diagram in this order to fix the labels: start at A, run along leg a to C, then along leg b to B, then back along the hypotenuse from B to A, pausing at D to see the split into q and p.</p>
  <p>The altitude creates two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, inside the original. In triangle ACD, the hypotenuse is leg a and one leg is projection p. In triangle CBD, the hypotenuse is leg b and one leg is projection q.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-main-triangle.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with right angle at C, altitude CD to hypotenuse AB, legs a and b, hypotenuse c, projections p and q, and altitude h labeled" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Main triangle ABC: altitude CD splits hypotenuse AB into projection p (AD) and projection q (DB).</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Do the Projection Theorems Work?</h2>
  <p>The proof rests on similar triangles. Drawing altitude CD divides triangle ABC into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, and both are similar to the original triangle. Similarity means their matching sides stay in the same ratio, and one of those ratios gives the projection theorem directly.</p>
  <p>Start with leg a. Triangle ACD shares the angle at A with triangle ABC, and both contain a right angle, so their third angles must match as well. Matching the sides that sit in the same positions gives the proportion:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{AD}{AC} = \\frac{AC}{AB}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now substitute the standard symbols: <span set:html={rich('[[AD = p]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[AC = a]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[AB = c]]')} />. The proportion becomes:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{p}{a} = \\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cross-multiplying clears the fractions: <span set:html={rich('[[p \\times c = a \\times a]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} />. The same argument applied to the other small triangle, CBD, gives <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, since triangle CBD shares the angle at B with the whole triangle. Similarity does all the work: matching sides of similar triangles stay proportional, and the projection theorem is what that proportion looks like after cross-multiplication.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-similar-triangles.svg')} alt="Original right triangle ABC beside smaller similar triangles ACD and CBD, showing the proportions p over a equals a over c and q over b equals b over c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Similar-triangles proof: p over a equals a over c, and q over b equals b over c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples Using All Five Calculator Modes</h2>
  <p>Each example states the given values, the mode, the substitution, and the final answer. Two examples cover each of the five modes, so you can see every mode twice with different numbers.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Find Projection P</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm, use mode 1, Hypotenuse Segment p. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9^2 / 15 = 81 / 15 = 5.4]]')} /> cm. </p>
  <h3>Example 2: Find Projection P</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} /> m, use mode 1. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[p = 12^2 / 20 = 144 / 20 = 7.2]]')} /> m. The other projection is 12.8 m, and <span set:html={rich('[[7.2 + 12.8 = 20]]')} /> m confirms the result.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Find Projection Q</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm, use mode 2, Hypotenuse Segment q. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[q = 12^2 / 15 = 144 / 15 = 9.6]]')} /> cm. This is the same triangle as Example 1, viewed from the other leg.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Find Projection Q</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> m, use mode 2. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[q = 8^2 / 10 = 64 / 10 = 6.4]]')} /> m. The other projection is 3.6 m, and this is the familiar 6-8-10 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Find Leg A</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm, use mode 3, Leg a Squared Projection. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = \\sqrt{225} = 15]]')} /> cm. The product is a perfect square, so the answer is exact, with no rounding needed.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Find Leg A</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm, use mode 3. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 4} = \\sqrt{40} \\approx 6.325]]')} /> cm. The product 40 is not a perfect square, so the answer is an approximation rounded to three decimal places.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Find Leg B</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, use mode 4, Leg b Squared Projection. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{400} = 20]]')} /> cm. This is the same triangle as Example 5, viewed from the other projection, and the two legs 15 and 20 complete the 15-20-25 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Find Leg B</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6]]')} /> cm, use mode 4. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{10 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{60} \\approx 7.746]]')} /> cm. The matching leg a is about 6.325 cm, from Example 6.</p>
  <h3>Example 9: Find Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, use mode 5, Projections Sum to c. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9 + 16 = 25]]')} /> cm. No squaring and no square root are needed, since the two segments simply join into the whole side.</p>
  <h3>Example 10: Find Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.5]]')} /> m, use mode 5. The formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 3.5 + 6.5 = 10]]')} /> m. Decimal projections add the same way as whole numbers, so line the decimal points up and add.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-all-measurements -->
<section id="sec-all-measurements" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate All Main Measurements of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Knowing the two projections is enough to rebuild the entire triangle. Use <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm. First, the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9 + 16 = 25]]')} /> cm. Then leg a is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = 15]]')} /> cm, and leg b is <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 \\times 16} = 20]]')} /> cm. The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{9 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{144} = 12]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>One pair of numbers yields four lengths: c, a, b, and h. That is the power of the projection relationships: two segments on the hypotenuse carry the full shape of the triangle. A final check closes the loop: <span set:html={rich('[[15^2 + 20^2 = 225 + 400 = 625 = 25^2]]')} />, so the Pythagorean theorem confirms the whole triangle at once.</p>
  <p>Keep in mind that these are mathematical relationships, not separate calculator modes. This page calculates one value per mode. Rebuilding the whole triangle means running one mode, then using its result in the next, or working the steps by hand as shown above.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-numerical-example.svg')} alt="Right triangle with projections p equals 9 and q equals 16, hypotenuse 25, legs 15 and 20, and altitude 12, showing all six measurements on one diagram" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">One pair of projections rebuilds the triangle: c = 25, a = 15, b = 20, h = 12.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projections and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The projection theorem and the Pythagorean theorem agree exactly, and the algebra shows why. Start from the two projection formulas:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 = c \\times p]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Add the matching formula for leg b, <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, to get:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c \\times p + c \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factoring out c from the right side gives <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c(p + q)]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} />, substitution yields the familiar result:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A numerical check with the 9-16-25 triangle confirms it: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 225 + 400 = 625]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c^2 = 25^2 = 625]]')} />. The two theorems are not rivals. The projection theorem splits the Pythagorean relationship into one piece per leg, and adding the pieces rebuilds it. That is why every result on this page can be checked with the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-altitude -->
<section id="sec-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projections and Altitude</h2>
  <p>The altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h = \\sqrt{p \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm. The product is <span set:html={rich('[[9 \\times 16 = 144]]')} />, and the square root of 144 is 12, so <span set:html={rich('[[h = 12]]')} /> cm. The altitude is the perpendicular segment from the right angle to the hypotenuse, and its length is fixed once p and q are known. This is the same geometric mean idea that links legs to projections, applied to the altitude instead.</p>
  <p>Note that this page does not have a dedicated altitude mode. The calculator returns projections, legs, and the hypotenuse, but not h. For a page that solves the altitude directly, use the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-projections-calculator/">altitude from projections calculator</a>, which takes p and q and returns h. The <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-calculator/">altitude calculator</a> covers other ways to find h, from the legs or from the area.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Projection Calculations</h2>
  <p>Four independent checks catch almost every error. Run at least one of them on every answer, and run all four when the problem matters. Each check uses a different relationship, so an error that slips past one is usually caught by another.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Projection Addition</h3>
  <p>Confirm that <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[9 + 16 = 25]]')} />, so the segments fit. If the sum differs from c, one of the projections is wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Projection Theorem for Leg A</h3>
  <p>Square leg a and compare it with <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times p]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = 225]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times p = 225]]')} />, so the answer passes. If the two numbers differ, the leg or the projection is wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Projection Theorem for Leg B</h3>
  <p>Square leg b and compare it with <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times q]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = 400]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times q = 400]]')} />, so the answer passes. Use this check whenever your answer is a leg.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm that <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[225 + 400 = 625 = 25^2]]')} />. This check tests the whole triangle at once, not just one formula, so it is the strongest of the four.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using a Right Triangle Projection Calculator</h2>
  <p>Most mistakes below trace back to labeling, not arithmetic, so relabel the diagram before recalculating when an answer looks wrong.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confusing projection p with projection q: p sits next to A and belongs to leg a, while q sits next to B and belongs to leg b.</li>
    <li>Pairing leg a with q instead of p: leg a uses p, so <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} />. Using q returns leg b, not leg a.</li>
    <li>Pairing leg b with p instead of q: leg b uses q, so <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]')} />. Using p returns leg a, not leg b.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the square root when calculating a leg: the product cp is a squared, not a. With c = 25 and p = 9, the leg is 15 cm, not 225.</li>
    <li>Mixing different measurement units: convert first. Multiplying meters by centimeters gives a meaningless product, so put both inputs in one unit.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong calculator mode: match the mode to your known values with the table near the top of this guide.</li>
    <li>Treating p or q as a perpendicular leg: projections lie flat along the hypotenuse. They are segments of the hypotenuse, not sides of the triangle.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that c = p + q: the two projections must add to the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Using invalid or negative lengths: lengths are positive, so a negative entry signals a data error, and zero projections describe no real triangle.</li>
    <li>Rounding too early: keep full precision until the final step, then round once.</li>
    <li>Applying the formulas to an arbitrary triangle: the theorem needs a right angle and its altitude, so without one there are no projections p and q in this sense.</li>
    <li>Confusing squared side lengths with actual side lengths: 225 is a squared, while 15 is a. Say which one you mean before writing a number down.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Cases and Important Projection Properties</h2>
  <p>Six properties follow directly from the projection formulas. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Equal projections produce equal legs. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = q = 5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} />, both legs equal <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{50} \\approx 7.071]]')} />, and the triangle is an isosceles right triangle.</li>
    <li>Unequal projections produce unequal legs: the longer projection belongs to the longer leg.</li>
    <li>Swapping p and q swaps the corresponding leg lengths but does not change the total hypotenuse, since <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} /> either way.</li>
    <li>Scaling all measurements by the same positive factor scales every result by that factor. Doubling p and q doubles c, a, b, and h.</li>
    <li>Projections are always positive and always smaller than c in a valid nondegenerate right triangle. A projection equal to c would leave the other projection at zero.</li>
    <li>The longer leg always casts the longer projection. Comparing leg lengths with projection lengths is a quick way to spot a swapped label.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The diagram below shows both cases with the hypotenuse kept at 10. Equal projections split it evenly and give equal legs, while unequal projections give legs of different lengths.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles with hypotenuse 10: one with equal projections p and q of 5 giving equal legs, and one with p equals 2 and q equals 8 giving unequal legs" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Equal projections give equal legs; unequal projections give unequal legs, with the same hypotenuse of 10.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 22: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Right Triangle Projection Relationships</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where projection problems test whether students can label a diagram and apply <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} /> instead of reaching for the Pythagorean theorem by habit. Similar-triangle exercises use the same setup, since the proof itself is an exercise in matching corresponding sides.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing uses the theorem when a right triangle must be rebuilt from partial measurements: if a drawing gives the hypotenuse and the foot of the altitude, the missing leg follows without remeasuring the shape.</p>
  <p>One condition limits every application: the required right-triangle geometry must actually exist. The theorem describes exact geometric relationships, not approximate field measurements, so it never replaces careful surveying or professional engineering measurement. Within its proper setting, though, it turns two known lengths into a third with a single multiplication and a square root.</p>
</section>

